sequenceDiagram
    %% @spec walkthrough/2
    %% @title Place an order
    %% @trigger The shopper presses "Place order" on the checkout page of the demo store.
    %% @scope From the click to the confirmation email. Signing in and browsing products are outside this flow.
    %% @summary One request crosses every backend layer once. Stock is held in Redis before the order is saved, and the confirmation email is sent later by a worker.
    %% @term hold: a short-lived Redis key that keeps stock aside for one order until the hold expires.
    %% @term minor units: money kept as whole paise or cents (₹6197.00 is 619700), so sums never lose fractions.
    %% @term DTO: data transfer object, the typed shape a request body or a reply must have.
    %% @term bearer token: a secret string sent as "Authorization: Bearer <token>" that stands for a signed-in session.
    %% @layer U kind=person The shopper, signed in as ada@example.com.
    %% @layer PG kind=client Pages: the checkout page, which reads the cart and runs the submit.
    %% @layer CMP kind=client Components: pure functions that render the cart summary and the confirmation.
    %% @layer ST kind=client Store: the observable cart state with items, address and status.
    %% @layer API kind=client API Client: builds the HTTP request and maps each status code to a typed result.
    %% @layer APP kind=app App: wires the in-memory infrastructure and hands every request to the router.
    %% @layer RT kind=router Routes: POST /api/orders with its middleware chain and controller.
    %% @layer MW kind=middleware Middleware: requestId on every request and requireUser on this route.
    %% @layer VAL kind=validator Validator: checks the body field by field and shapes it into the DTO.
    %% @layer CTL kind=controller Controller: calls the service and picks the status code.
    %% @layer SVC kind=service Service: prices the items, holds stock, saves the order and announces it.
    %% @layer REPO kind=repository Repository: inserts the order and its items in one transaction.
    %% @layer DB kind=db Database: in-memory tables orders, order_items, products and sessions.
    %% @layer SER kind=serializer Serializer: the public order shape, without internal ids.
    %% @layer DTO kind=dto DTO: PlaceOrderDto going in, OrderResponseDto coming out.
    %% @layer RD kind=cache Redis: stock counters and per-order holds with a TTL.
    %% @layer EB kind=eventbus Event Bus: fans out order.placed to whoever subscribed.
    %% @layer Q kind=queue Mail Queue: confirmation jobs waiting for the worker.
    %% @layer WK kind=worker Mail Worker: takes one job and sends the email.
    %% @layer MAIL kind=external Mail: the mail service, an in-memory stand-in here.
    autonumber
    actor U as Shopper
    participant PG as Pages
    participant CMP as Components
    participant ST as Store
    participant API as API Client
    participant APP as App
    participant RT as Routes
    participant MW as Middleware
    participant VAL as Validator
    participant CTL as Controller
    participant SVC as Service
    participant REPO as Repository
    participant DB as Database
    participant SER as Serializer
    participant DTO as DTO
    participant RD as Redis
    participant EB as Event Bus
    participant Q as Mail Queue
    participant WK as Mail Worker
    participant MAIL as Mail

    %% @section Check the request
    %% @summary The click becomes an HTTP request, and the sign-in and the body are checked before any work starts.
    %% @step The shopper submits the checkout form, and the page starts the submit.
    %% @uses hook useCart src/frontend/hooks/useCart.ts:14 Adds and removes items and totals the cart before checkout.
    %% @ref src/frontend/pages/CheckoutPage.ts:44
    U->>PG: Click "Place order"
    %% @step The page takes the cart items and the shipping address out of the store and marks the status as submitting.
    %% @payload.result { "items": [{ "productId": "p_1", "quantity": 1 }, { "productId": "p_2", "quantity": 2 }], "address": { "line1": "1 Lovelace Lane", "city": "Pune", "postcode": "411001" } }
    %% @ref src/frontend/pages/CheckoutPage.ts:45
    PG->>ST: getState() and setState(submitting)
    %% @step The page hands the body and the session token to the API client.
    %% @ref src/frontend/pages/CheckoutPage.ts:47
    PG->>API: placeOrder(body, token)
    %% @step The client posts the order as JSON with the bearer token in a header.
    %% @payload.headers Authorization: Bearer demo-token
    %% @payload.headers Content-Type: application/json
    %% @payload.body { "items": [{ "productId": "p_1", "quantity": 1 }, { "productId": "p_2", "quantity": 2 }], "shippingAddress": { "line1": "1 Lovelace Lane", "city": "Pune", "postcode": "411001" } }
    %% @ref src/frontend/api/client.ts:50
    API->>APP: POST /api/orders
    %% @step The app catches any unexpected error and otherwise lets the router match the method and path.
    %% @ref src/backend/app/app.ts:81
    APP->>RT: dispatch(request)
    %% @step The route runs its chain in order: the request id middleware, then the sign-in check, then the validator, then the controller.
    %% @ref src/backend/routes/orders.routes.ts:15
    RT->>MW: requestId, then requireUser
    %% @step The middleware reads the bearer token from the Authorization header and looks the session up.
    %% @payload.sql -- an in-memory table, doing the job of: SELECT user_id, email FROM sessions WHERE id = 'demo-token'
    %% @ref src/backend/middleware/auth.middleware.ts:12
    %% @ref src/backend/app/app.ts:73
    MW->>DB: sessions.findById("demo-token")
    %% @step The session row names the user, and the middleware puts them on the request context.
    %% @payload.result { "id": "u_1", "email": "ada@example.com" }
    %% @ref src/backend/app/app.ts:73
    DB-->>MW: { userId: "u_1", email: "ada@example.com" }
    alt Signed in
        %% @step With a user on the context the chain moves on to the validator.
        %% @ref src/backend/middleware/auth.middleware.ts:16-17
        MW->>VAL: next() → placeOrderValidator
    else No or unknown token
        %% @step Without a valid bearer token the chain stops in the middleware, and the shopper gets a plain message.
        %% @payload.body { "error": "Sign in to place an order" }
        %% @ref src/backend/middleware/auth.middleware.ts:15
        APP-->>API: 401 Unauthorized
    end
    %% @step The validator parses the JSON body and checks every field, collecting all problems at once instead of stopping at the first.
    %% @schema items non-empty array → 422 "must be a non-empty array"
    %% @schema items.<i>.productId non-empty string → 422 "must be a non-empty string"
    %% @schema items.<i>.quantity whole number greater than 0 → 422 "must be a whole number greater than 0"
    %% @schema shippingAddress.line1 non-empty string → 422 "must be a non-empty string"
    %% @schema shippingAddress.city non-empty string → 422 "must be a non-empty string"
    %% @schema shippingAddress.postcode non-empty string → 422 "must be a non-empty string"
    %% @ref src/backend/validators/placeOrder.validator.ts:61
    VAL->>VAL: validatePlaceOrder(body)
    alt Valid body
        %% @step The checked values become the typed DTO that the rest of the backend trusts.
        %% @payload.result { "items": [{ "productId": "p_1", "quantity": 1 }, { "productId": "p_2", "quantity": 2 }], "shippingAddress": { "line1": "1 Lovelace Lane", "city": "Pune", "postcode": "411001" } }
        %% @ref src/backend/dto/placeOrder.dto.ts:15
        %% @ref src/backend/validators/placeOrder.validator.ts:64
        VAL->>DTO: PlaceOrderDto
        %% @step The validator calls the next handler, the controller, with the user and the DTO on the context.
        %% @ref src/backend/validators/placeOrder.validator.ts:65
        VAL->>CTL: next() → placeOrder(ctx)
    else Invalid body
        %% @step A bad body ends the request with the full list of field problems, so the form can mark every one.
        %% @payload.body { "error": "Order details are invalid", "fields": { "items.0.quantity": "must be a whole number greater than 0", "shippingAddress.city": "must be a non-empty string" } }
        %% @ref src/backend/validators/placeOrder.validator.ts:63
        APP-->>API: 422 Unprocessable Entity
    end

    %% @section Reserve stock
    %% @summary Every line is priced, then its stock is held in Redis so two shoppers cannot buy the last item.
    %% @step The controller asks the service to place the order and will turn its outcome into a status code.
    %% @ref src/backend/controllers/orders.controller.ts:21
    CTL->>SVC: placeOrder(user, dto)
    %% @step Each line is priced from the products table. An unknown product id ends in a 404.
    %% @uses util sumLines src/backend/utils/money.ts:24 Adds up unit price times quantity in minor units.
    %% @ref src/backend/services/orders.service.ts:96
    SVC->>REPO: findById(productId) for each line
    %% @step The product rows give the names and unit prices the order will carry.
    %% @payload.result { "id": "p_1", "name": "Mechanical keyboard", "unitPrice": 499900, "stock": 10 }
    %% @ref src/backend/repositories/products.repository.ts:12
    REPO-->>SVC: product rows
    %% @step For each line the service moves units from the stock counter into a hold key with a TTL, so a crash or an abandoned order gives the stock back on its own.
    %% @payload.input stock:p_1 = 10 → DECRBY 1, then SET hold:<orderId>:p_1 1 EX 900
    %% @uses config RESERVATION_TTL_SECONDS src/backend/config/config.ts:18 How long a hold lasts before the cron returns the stock (900 s).
    %% @uses cron releaseExpiredReservations src/backend/cron/releaseExpiredReservations.cron.ts:13 Runs every second and returns expired holds to the stock counter.
    %% @ref src/backend/services/orders.service.ts:111
    %% @ref src/backend/infra/memoryRedis.ts:57
    SVC->>RD: reserve(stock:p_1, hold:<orderId>:p_1, 1, 900)
    alt Stock held
        %% @step The counter had enough units, so the hold exists now and the next line is tried.
        %% @state before stock:p_1 = 10, no hold
        %% @state after stock:p_1 = 9, hold:<orderId>:p_1 = 1 for 900 s
        %% @ref src/backend/infra/memoryRedis.ts:62
        RD-->>SVC: true
    else Stock short
        %% @step The counter was below the quantity, so nothing changed in Redis.
        %% @ref src/backend/infra/memoryRedis.ts:59
        RD-->>SVC: false
        %% @step The service gives earlier holds of this order back and stops with OutOfStockError.
        %% @effect Earlier holds of this order are released
        %% @ref src/backend/services/orders.service.ts:114
        SVC-->>CTL: OutOfStockError
        %% @step The controller maps the error to a conflict, naming the product so the page can show which line failed.
        %% @payload.body { "error": "\"Limited edition poster\" is out of stock", "productId": "p_3" }
        %% @ref src/backend/controllers/orders.controller.ts:25
        APP-->>API: 409 Conflict
    end

    %% @section Save the order
    %% @summary The order and its lines are written in one transaction, and the event that triggers the email is published.
    %% @step With every line held, the service builds the order record and asks the repository to save it with its items.
    %% @payload.input { "id": "<uuid>", "orderNumber": "SW-1001", "userId": "u_1", "status": "placed", "currency": "INR", "total": 619700, "shippingAddress": { "line1": "1 Lovelace Lane", "city": "Pune", "postcode": "411001" }, "placedAt": "2026-01-01T00:00:00.000Z" }
    %% @field orderNumber from the repository's counter, SW-1001 for the first order
    %% @field total from sumLines over the priced items, in minor units
    %% @ref src/backend/services/orders.service.ts:84
    SVC->>REPO: insert(order, items)
    %% @step The repository writes the order row and one row per line inside a single transaction, so a failure leaves no half-saved order.
    %% @payload.sql -- in-memory tables, doing the job of: INSERT INTO orders (id, order_number, user_id, status, currency, total, shipping_address, placed_at) VALUES (...)
    %% @payload.sql -- then, in the same transaction: INSERT INTO order_items (id, order_id, product_id, name, unit_price, quantity) VALUES (...)
    %% @state before stock held in Redis, no order row
    %% @state after one orders row and two order_items rows
    %% @ref src/backend/repositories/orders.repository.ts:19
    REPO->>DB: transaction: insert orders, insert order_items
    %% @step The saved rows come back as written.
    %% @ref src/backend/infra/memoryDb.ts:11
    DB-->>REPO: rows
    %% @step The repository returns the order with its items.
    %% @ref src/backend/repositories/orders.repository.ts:18
    REPO-->>SVC: { order, items }
    %% @step The service announces the order. Nothing in this request waits for the email, so the reply is not slowed down.
    %% @async The subscriber only queues a job, the email is sent later
    %% @payload.event { "type": "order.placed", "orderId": "<uuid>", "orderNumber": "SW-1001", "email": "ada@example.com", "total": 619700, "currency": "INR" }
    %% @effect A confirmation job is queued for the mail worker
    %% @ref src/backend/services/orders.service.ts:85
    SVC->>EB: publish order.placed
    %% @step The service returns the saved order to the controller.
    %% @ref src/backend/services/orders.service.ts:93
    SVC-->>CTL: { order, items }

    %% @section Reply
    %% @summary The order is serialised into its public shape, the shopper sees the confirmation.
    %% @step The controller asks the serializer for the public shape of the order.
    %% @ref src/backend/controllers/orders.controller.ts:22
    CTL->>SER: serializeOrder(placed)
    %% @step The serializer builds the response DTO: order number, items, total in minor units and as text, address and time. Internal ids and the user id stay out.
    %% @ref src/backend/serializers/order.serializer.ts:6
    %% @ref src/backend/dto/placeOrder.dto.ts:30
    SER->>DTO: OrderResponseDto
    %% @step The public order comes back to the controller.
    %% @payload.result { "orderNumber": "SW-1001", "status": "placed", "currency": "INR", "items": [{ "productId": "p_1", "name": "Mechanical keyboard", "quantity": 1, "unitPrice": 499900, "lineTotal": 499900 }, { "productId": "p_2", "name": "USB-C cable", "quantity": 2, "unitPrice": 59900, "lineTotal": 119800 }], "total": 619700, "totalDisplay": "₹6197.00", "shippingAddress": { "line1": "1 Lovelace Lane", "city": "Pune", "postcode": "411001" }, "placedAt": "2026-01-01T00:00:00.000Z" }
    %% @ref src/backend/serializers/order.serializer.ts:6
    SER-->>CTL: public order
    %% @step The controller answers 201 with the public order. On the way out the request id middleware adds the X-Request-Id header.
    %% @payload.headers X-Request-Id: 7f3c…
    %% @payload.body { "orderNumber": "SW-1001", "status": "placed", "currency": "INR", "items": [{ "productId": "p_1", "name": "Mechanical keyboard", "quantity": 1, "unitPrice": 499900, "lineTotal": 499900 }, { "productId": "p_2", "name": "USB-C cable", "quantity": 2, "unitPrice": 59900, "lineTotal": 119800 }], "total": 619700, "totalDisplay": "₹6197.00", "shippingAddress": { "line1": "1 Lovelace Lane", "city": "Pune", "postcode": "411001" }, "placedAt": "2026-01-01T00:00:00.000Z" }
    %% @ref src/backend/controllers/orders.controller.ts:22
    %% @ref src/backend/middleware/requestId.middleware.ts:10
    APP-->>API: 201 Created
    %% @step The client turns the status code into a typed result, so the page never looks at raw HTTP.
    %% @payload.result { "kind": "placed", "order": { "orderNumber": "SW-1001", "total": 619700 } }
    %% @ref src/frontend/api/client.ts:26
    API-->>PG: { kind: "placed", order }
    %% @step The page stores the placed order and empties the cart.
    %% @state before status submitting, two items in the cart
    %% @state after status placed with the order, cart empty
    %% @ref src/frontend/pages/CheckoutPage.ts:55
    PG->>ST: setState({ status: placed, items: [] })
    %% @step The page asks the component for the confirmation markup.
    %% @ref src/frontend/components/OrderConfirmation.ts:5
    PG->>CMP: renderOrderConfirmation(order)
    %% @step The component returns the thank-you block with the order number and the total.
    %% @ref src/frontend/components/OrderConfirmation.ts:11
    CMP-->>PG: <h2>Thank you! Order SW-1001 is placed.</h2>
    %% @step The shopper sees the confirmation.
    %% @ref src/frontend/pages/CheckoutPage.ts:28
    PG-->>U: Confirmation page

    %% @section Send the email
    %% @summary Background work: the event becomes a queued job, the worker picks it up and the email goes out.
    %% @step The app subscribed to order.placed at start-up. Its handler turns the event into a small job and queues it.
    %% @async Runs when the event is published, outside the request
    %% @payload.event { "to": "ada@example.com", "orderNumber": "SW-1001", "total": 619700, "currency": "INR" }
    %% @ref src/backend/app/app.ts:56
    %% @ref src/backend/workers/orderConfirmation.worker.ts:16
    EB->>Q: enqueue(confirmationJob)
    %% @step The worker runs every second and takes one job off the queue.
    %% @ref src/backend/workers/orderConfirmation.worker.ts:38
    WK->>Q: dequeue()
    %% @step The queue hands over the oldest job, or nothing when it is empty.
    %% @ref src/backend/infra/memoryQueue.ts:10
    Q-->>WK: job
    %% @step The worker formats the total and sends the confirmation email.
    %% @uses util formatMoney src/backend/utils/money.ts:16 Turns 619700 INR into ₹6197.00.
    %% @payload.body To: ada@example.com, Subject: Sequence Walkthrough Demo Store: order SW-1001 confirmed, Text: Thanks for your order SW-1001. Total: ₹6197.00. We will ship it soon.
    %% @effect Confirmation email sent to ada@example.com
    %% @ref src/backend/workers/orderConfirmation.worker.ts:41
    WK->>MAIL: send(to, subject, text)
    %% @step The mail service confirms the send. In the demo it keeps the mail in memory and prints it.
    %% @ref src/backend/infra/mailer.ts:19
    MAIL-->>WK: sent
